  schema="HowTo"
  howToSteps={[
    { name: "Step 1 — Select your device type (iPhone, iPad, or Android)", text: "Choose your specific device so the guide loads the exact setup steps for your device's buttons and settings menus." },
    { name: "Step 2 — Choose your preferred video call application", text: "Select FaceTime (Apple devices), Zoom, Google Meet, or WhatsApp Video based on what your family or doctor uses." },
    { name: "Step 3 — Follow the large-text step-by-step installation and settings instructions", text: "Each step shows exactly which buttons to tap and what to look for on your screen, written in plain English." },
    { name: "Step 4 — Make a test call with a family member or friend", text: "Practice making and answering a video call with someone you trust before your first telehealth or family video call." }
  ]}
>

  <!-- ELEMENT 2 — HERO -->
  <div class="svc-hero">
    <div class="svc-breadcrumb">
      <a href="/tools/" class="svc-breadcrumb-link">Tools &amp; Calculators</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true"> › </span>
      <span>Smartphone Video Call Setup Guide for Seniors</span>
    </div>

    <h1 class="svc-h1">
      Smartphone Video Call Setup Guide for Seniors — FaceTime, Zoom &amp; Google Meet Step by Step
    </h1>
    <p class="svc-date">Video call platform instructions verified against current iOS 17, Android 14, and Zoom v6.x in {lastReviewed}.</p>

    <div class="svc-aeo-block" role="note" aria-label="Quick Answer">
      <p class="svc-aeo-label">Quick Answer</p>
      <p class="svc-aeo-text">
        <strong class="svc-gold">FaceTime</strong> (iPhone/iPad users) is the easiest video call option—it requires no separate account and works with one tap from your Contacts. <strong class="svc-gold">Zoom</strong> and <strong class="svc-gold">Google Meet</strong> work on all devices (iPhone, Android, iPad, Fire tablet) and are free. For telehealth appointments, your doctor's office will send you a specific link to tap — you do not need any technical knowledge to join.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="svc-problem">
      Video calling is one of the most important social connection tools available to seniors who live alone or far from family. Studies from the National Academies of Sciences show that social isolation increases the risk of premature death by 26% and is associated with higher rates of depression and cognitive decline. Yet millions of older Americans avoid video calls because setup instructions are written for younger users. This guide walks through setup using plain English, large text, and exact button-by-button instructions for every common device.
    </p>

    <ul class="svc-who-list" aria-label="Who this tool is for">
      <li class="svc-who-item"><span class="svc-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span> Seniors who want to make their first video call to family members or grandchildren and need simple, step-by-step setup instructions.</li>
      <li class="svc-who-item"><span class="svc-who-item">→ Seniors who need to join telehealth medical appointments by video and are unsure how to download, open, or use Zoom or their healthcare system's video app.</span></li>
      <li class="svc-who-item"><span class="svc-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span> Adult children or caregivers helping an older parent set up their first video calling capability alongside our <a href="/tools/telehealth-how-it-works/" class="svc-inline-link">Telehealth How It Works explainer</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- ELEMENT 3 — TOOL -->
  <div class="svc-tool-block" id="guide">
    <SmartphoneSetupGuide client:load />
  </div>

  <NoSignupStatement />

  <!-- ELEMENT 4 — HOW TO USE -->
  <section class="svc-section" aria-labelledby="how-to-heading">
    <h2 id="how-to-heading">How to Set Up Video Calling on Your Device — Step by Step</h2>

    <p class="svc-intro-p">
      Setting up video calling for the first time takes about 10 to 15 minutes. You only need to do this setup once — after that, every video call takes just one or two taps.
    </p>

    <ol class="svc-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Step 1 — Make sure your device is connected to Wi-Fi.</strong>
        Look at the top of your phone or tablet screen for the Wi-Fi symbol (it looks like curved lines, similar to a fan shape). If you do not see the Wi-Fi symbol, go to Settings → Wi-Fi → turn it on → tap your home network name → enter your Wi-Fi password. Video calls use large amounts of data and work best over Wi-Fi.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 2 — Install the correct app for your call.</strong>
        If you have an iPhone or iPad, FaceTime is already built in — look for the green icon with a white video camera. For Zoom: open the App Store (iPhone/iPad) or Google Play Store (Android) → type "Zoom" in the search bar → tap Install → tap Open when done. For Google Meet: search "Google Meet" in the same stores and install the same way.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 3 — Make your screen text larger and easier to see.</strong>
        iPhone/iPad: Tap Settings (the gray gear icon) → Accessibility → Display &amp; Text Size → Larger Text → drag the slider all the way right. Android: Tap Settings → Accessibility → Font Size → move to Large. This does not change any calling settings — it just makes all text on your phone bigger.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Step 4 — Practice with a family member or friend before your first real call.</strong>
        Ask a family member to call you on FaceTime or send you a Zoom meeting link. When your phone rings or you receive the link, tap the green Answer button or tap the Zoom link. Let the family member guide you through the first call so you are comfortable before any important telehealth visit.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="svc-pro-tip">
      <p class="svc-tip-label">💡 Pro Tip — For Telehealth Appointments, Your Doctor Sends the Link</p>
      <p class="svc-tip-text">
        <strong>You do not need to set anything up in advance for most telehealth appointments.</strong> When your doctor schedules a telehealth visit, they email or text you a specific link to tap. Tap the link, tap "Join" or "Join as Guest," and your doctor will see you automatically. You do not need a Zoom account, username, or password to join a telehealth video visit as a patient. Learn more with our <a href="/tools/telehealth-how-it-works/" class="svc-inline-link">Telehealth How It Works guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Terminology Table -->
    <div class="svc-table-section">
      <h3>Video Calling &amp; Smartphone Technology Terms Explained</h3>
      <p class="svc-table-intro">
        Video calling guides often use technology terms that are confusing without explanation. Review this table to understand exactly what each term means before you start.
      </p>
      <div class="svc-table-wrap">
        <table class="svc-ref-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Term / Abbreviation</th><th>Full Official Name</th><th>Detailed Plain-English Definition</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>FaceTime</strong></td>
              <td>Apple FaceTime Video Calling</td>
              <td>A free video and audio calling application built into all Apple iPhones and iPads. Requires both callers to have Apple devices (iPhone, iPad, or Mac). Works over Wi-Fi or cellular data at no extra cost beyond your existing phone plan.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="svc-row-alt">
              <td><strong>Wi-Fi</strong></td>
              <td>Wireless Internet Connection</td>
              <td>A wireless internet connection from your home router. When your phone is connected to Wi-Fi, it uses your home internet to make video calls at no additional cost. Video calls over Wi-Fi are higher quality than calls over cellular data.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Data / Cellular Data</strong></td>
              <td>Mobile Data Connection</td>
              <td>Your phone's internet connection through your phone carrier (AT&T, Verizon, T-Mobile, etc.) when not connected to Wi-Fi. Video calls use approximately 600 MB of data per hour, which can result in overage charges if you have a limited data plan.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="svc-row-alt">
              <td><strong>App / Application</strong></td>
              <td>Software Application</td>
              <td>A program installed on your smartphone or tablet that provides a specific function. Zoom, FaceTime, Google Meet, and WhatsApp are all apps.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>App Store / Play Store</strong></td>
              <td>Apple App Store / Google Play Store</td>
              <td>The official online marketplace for downloading apps (software programs) to your device. The App Store is used by iPhones and iPads. The Google Play Store is used by Android phones and tablets.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="svc-row-alt">
              <td><strong>Front-Facing Camera</strong></td>
              <td>Selfie Camera / Front Camera</td>
              <td>The camera on the front of your phone or tablet (the same side as the screen) that films your face during a video call. The rear camera (on the back of the phone) is used for photos and is not the correct camera for video calls.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Mute</strong></td>
              <td>Audio Mute Function</td>
              <td>A button during a video call that turns off your microphone so the other person cannot hear you. Your video image is still visible. Tap Mute again to turn your microphone back on. Look for a microphone icon with a line through it.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

