        Raymond's cardiologist offered a telehealth checkup via Zoom. Raymond had never used Zoom and was nervous about missing the appointment.
      </p>
      <table class="svc-scenario-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Telehealth Preparation Step</th><th>Details &amp; Outcome</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Doctor's Office Sent Zoom Link by Text</td><td>Link arrived 30 minutes before appointment — Raymond saved it</td></tr>
          <tr class="svc-row-alt"><td>Zoom App Installed in Advance</td><td class="svc-yes">✅ Son helped install Zoom app 2 days before appointment during practice session</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tapped Link at Appointment Time</td><td class="svc-yes">✅ Zoom opened automatically; tapped "Join as Guest" — no username or password required</td></tr>
          <tr class="svc-row-alt"><td>Appointment Duration &amp; Quality</td><td class="svc-yes">✅ 20-minute telehealth visit completed successfully — Raymond reports "easier than a phone call"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="svc-result-block">
        <span class="svc-result-highlight">✅ Raymond Completed His Telehealth Appointment Without Any Technical Assistance</span>
        The key was installing Zoom two days early and practicing the "tap the link" step once before the real appointment.
      </div>
      <p class="svc-action">
        <strong>Takeaway:</strong> Always install the video app at least two days before a telehealth appointment. Tap the doctor's link once with a family member present to practice.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="svc-disclaimer">
      Representative setup scenarios based on documented senior technology adoption patterns from AARP and Pew Research Center.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ELEMENT 6 — PITFALLS -->
  <section class="svc-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common Video Call Setup Mistakes — And How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Trying to Install a Video App Without a Wi-Fi Connection</h3>
    <p>
      App downloads (Zoom, Google Meet, WhatsApp) require an internet connection. Downloading apps over cellular data can consume 50 to 200 MB per download and may count against your monthly data allowance.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The fix is</strong> to always connect to your home Wi-Fi network (the fan-shaped icon at the top of your screen) before installing any new app.
    </p>

    <h3>Using the Rear Camera Instead of the Front Camera During a Video Call</h3>
    <p>
      Many seniors accidentally tap the camera flip button during a call and switch to the rear camera, which shows the ceiling or wall instead of their face. The other person can no longer see you.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The fix is</strong> to look for a camera icon with two arrows (the flip camera button) during any video call. Tap it once if you are accidentally showing the wrong view—it will flip back to the front-facing (selfie) camera.
    </p>

    <h3>Falling for Fake Zoom Meeting Invitation Scams</h3>
    <p>
      Scammers send fake Zoom meeting links via email or text message that install malware or steal account information. Seniors are frequently targeted with fake "Medicare video appointment" or "bank security review" meeting invitations.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The fix is</strong> to only join video calls from links sent directly by your doctor's office, a known family member, or a friend. If you receive an unexpected video call invitation from an unknown sender, do not tap the link. Review our <a href="/tools/scam-message-call-decoder/" class="svc-inline-link">Scam Message &amp; Call Decoder</a> to identify suspicious messages.
    </p>
  </section>

